By Proposition \ref{prop:linear basis in F world}, the chain complex $C_*(\Sigma_{\cW})$ is
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = 3em]
    0 \rar & 
    \displaystyle\bigoplus_{W \subseteq V} \QQ\sigma_W \arrow[r,"d_{3}"] &
    \displaystyle\bigoplus_{v_1, v_2} \QQ \sigma_{v_1, v_2} \arrow[r, "d_2"] & 
    \displaystyle\bigoplus_{v} \Q \sigma_v \arrow[r,"d_1"] & 
     \QQ \sigma_0 \arrow[r] & 0.
\end{tikzcd}
\]
The first nonzero vector space has a basis indexed by the 15 2-dimensional isotropic subspaces of $\F_2^4$.
The third nonzero vector space has a basis indexed by the 15 nonzero vectors in $V = (\F_2)^4$.
The second nonzero vector space has a basis indexed by the 45 choices of: one two-dimensional isotropic subspace together with 
two nonzero vectors $v_1,v_2$ contained in it. 

We see this as follows. For every $0 \ne v \in W$, we have $\sigma_v \in \{\sigma_{\alpha}^W \}$.
Since $W$ has exactly three nonzero vectors, the three $1$-dimensional cones in the admissible decomposition of $C(F_W)$ are exactly $\{\sigma_v \}_{v \in W}$. We may then identify the $2$-dimensional cones with those spanned by every pair of vectors, and we label them by $\sigma_{v_1, v_2}$. Note that the pair $v_1, v_2$ determines $W$, so there is no ambiguity in this notation.
These cones are all alternating as in Section \ref{subsection:alternating}.

Computing the homology, we find that the only non-vanishing homology is in degree $2$, with $H_2(C_*(\Sigma_{\cW})) \cong \Q^{16}$. Indeed, it is clear that $d_1$ is surjective, with kernel generated by $\alpha = \sigma_{v_1} - \sigma_{v_2}$ for vectors $v_1, v_2$. Let $W = \langle v_1, v_2 \rangle$ the subspace they generate. If it is isotropic, then $\alpha = \partial (\sigma_{v_1, v_2})$. 
Otherwise, $\dim W^{\perp} = 2$. Let $0 \ne v_3 \in W^{\perp}$. Then $\alpha = \partial (\sigma_{v_1, v_3} - \sigma_{v_2, v_3})$. This shows that $H_{0}(P) = H_1(P) = 0$, and as $H_3(P) = 0$, we have that $H_2(P)$ is the only term that might not vanish.  Counting dimensions, we obtain 
$$
\dim H_2(P) 
= \sum_{i=0}^3 (-1)^{i} \dim P_i
= 1-15+45-15 = 16
$$ 
Applying~\eqref{eq:comparison for Agm}, we obtain the result.
\end{proof}


\subsubsection{Computations for \texorpdfstring{$m>2$}{m > 2}}
\label{sec:mlarger2}

We now take $g = 2$ and $m > 2$. 
First, we understand the structure of $\PDrt_2/\GL_2(\Z)[m]$.


\begin{lemma}
     The perfect cone decomposition of $\PDrt_2/\GL_2(\Z)[m]$ contains
     \begin{center}
     \begin{tabular}{cl}
         $c_{3,m} := \frac{m^3}{6} \prod_{p \mid m} (1 - p^{-2})$  & 3-dimensional cones,  \\
         $c_{2,m} := \frac{m^3}{4} \prod_{p \mid m} (1 - p^{-2})$ & $2$-dimensional cones, \\ 
         $c_{1,m} := \frac{m^2}{2}\prod_{p \mid m} (1 - p^{-2})$ & rays.
     \end{tabular} 
          \end{center}
     \label{omegagcount}
    Moreover, these cones are all alternating.
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
In the perfect cone decomposition of $\PDrt_2$, the rays $R_v \coloneqq \R_{\geq 0} \cdot \langle vv^t\rangle$ are in bijection with primitive vectors $v \in \Z^2$, up to $\pm 1$. Two rays $R_{v_1}$ and $R_{v_2}$ are in the same $\GL_2(\Z)[m]$-orbit if and only if $v_1 \equiv \pm v_2$ (mod $m$). So, $\PDrt_2$ contains $\frac{m^2}{2}\prod_{p \mid m} (1 - p^{-2})$ distinct $\GL_2(\Z)[m]$-orbits of rays, indexed by  vectors $(x,y) \in (\Z/m\Z)^2$ with $\gcd(x,y,m) = 1$, up to $\pm 1$. Note that $(x, y) \in (\Z/m\Z)^2$ has a primitive representative in $\Z^2$ if and only if $\gcd(x,y,m) = 1$. Indeed, if $x \ne 0$, let $t \in \Z$ be such that $p \nmid t$ for all $p \mid (x,y)$, and $p \mid t$ for all $p \mid x$ such that $p \nmid (x,y)$. Then $\gcd(x, y + tm) = 1$.

Two rays $R_{v_1}$ and $R_{v_2}$ span a $2$-dimensional cone if and only if
$\det | v_1 \; v_2| = \pm 1$. Moreover, if $v_i,w_i \in \Z^2$ are primitive vectors such that $v_i \equiv w_i$ (mod $m$) and $\det | v_1 \; v_2| = \det | w_1 \; w_2| = \pm 1$, then there exists $g \in \GL_2(\Z)[m]$ such that $gv_i = w_i$, i.e., $g$ transforms $\langle R_{v_1}, R_{v_2}\rangle$ into $\langle R_{w_1}, R_{w_2}\rangle$. So the $\GL_2(\Z)[m]$-orbits of $2$-dimensional cones are in bijection with pairs of non-zero vectors $v_1,v_2 \in (\Z/m\Z)^2/\{\pm 1\}$ such that $\det | v_1 \; v_2| = \pm 1$ in $\Z/m\Z$. In particular, each ray in $\PDrt_2/\GL_2(\Z)[m]$ is contained in $m$ distinct $2$-dimensional cones, so $\PDrt_2/\GL_2(\Z)[m]$ contains $\frac{m^3}{4} \prod_{p \mid m} (1 - p^{-2})$ distinct $2$-dimensional cones.

Every $2$-dimensional cone $\langle R_{v_1}, R_{v_2}\rangle$ is contained in two full-dimensional cones:
$$\langle R_{v_1}, R_{v_2}, R_{v_1 + v_2}\rangle \;\; \text{ and } \;\; \langle R_{v_1}, R_{v_2}, R_{v_1 - v_2}\rangle.$$
Moreover, if $w_1,w_2 \in \Z^2$ as earlier, then the element $g \in \GL_2(\Z)[m]$ that transforms $\langle R_{v_1}, R_{v_2}\rangle$ into $\langle R_{w_1}, R_{w_2}\rangle$ will also transform the two full-dimensional cones containing $\langle R_{v_1}, R_{v_2}\rangle$ into the two full-dimensional cones containing $\langle R_{w_1}, R_{w_2}\rangle$. So $\PDrt_2/\GL_2(\Z)[m]$ contains $\frac{m^3}{6} \prod_{p \mid m} (1 - p^{-2})$ full-dimensional cones.

The $\GL_2(\Z)[m]$ action does not induce any non-trivial automorphisms of a cone $C$ because its minimal vectors are all distinct mod $m$, so the cones are all alternating.
\end{proof}


\begin{remark}
    Kolay \cite[Proof of Theorem 9.1]{kolay} determines the quotient of the  Farey complex on $\mathbb{H}$ by $\SL_2(\Z)[m]$. The \emph{Farey complex} on $\mathbb{H}$ is defined as follows. Given two rational numbers $a/b$ and $c/d$ (in irreducible form) in $\mathbb{Q} \subset \mathbb{R} \subset \mathbb{C}$, the Farey complex has a semicircle in the upper half plane connecting $a/b$ and $c/d$ if 
    $
    \det \left(
        (a , b),
        (c , d)
    \right ) = \pm 1.
    $
    By the proof of Lemma \ref{omegagcount}, the quotient of the Farey complex on $\mathbb{H}$ by $\SL_2(\Z)[m]$ has the same combinatorial structure as the link of $\PDrt_2/\GL_2(\Z)[m]$.
    For completeness, we include the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:genus} below, but it can also be deduced by the proof of Theorem 9.1 in \cite{kolay}.
\end{remark}


\begin{lemma} For $m\ge 3$, 
        the link of $\PDrt_2/\GL_2(\Z)[m]$ is homeomorphic to an orientable surface of genus 
        $
        1-\frac{1}{2}(c_{1,m} - c_{2,m} + c_{3,m}).
        $
        \label{lem:genus}
\end{lemma}



\begin{proof}
Pick a representative $C$ for each $\GL_2(\Z)[m]$-orbit of full-dimensional cones in $\PDrt_2$. Our $\GL_2(\Z)[m]$ action does not induce any non-trivial automorphisms of $C$ because its minimal vectors are all distinct mod $m$. So the link $L$ consists of one triangle for each chosen representative $C$, glued along common edges.

Smoothness along edges follows because each edge is contained in precisely two triangles. 
Smoothness at vertices follows because, near each ray corresponding to $v_1 \in (\Z/m\Z)^2\setminus 0$, the triangulated link $L$ looks like an $m$-gon with $v_1$ in the center, the $m$ vertices corresponding to 
    $$v_2,v_2 + v_1, v_2 + 2v_1, \dots , v_2 + (m-1)v_1 \in (\Z/m\Z)^2$$
    and edges connecting the center to each of the vertices. 

Finally, $L$ inherits an orientation from $\R^3 \supset \PDrt_2$ because the action of each $g\in \GL_2(\Z)[m]$ on $\R^3 \supset \PDrt_2$ is orientation-preserving. This is by the argument in \cite[Lemma 7.1]{elbaz-vincent-gangl-soule-perfect}, since all $g \in \GL_2(\Z)[m]$ will have positive determinant. 
    The genus can be calculated by computing the Euler characteristic using the numbers of vertices, faces, and edges in Lemma \ref{omegagcount}.
\end{proof}



