\title{Dynamics of Giant Atom Coupled with Disordered Lattices}


\author{Maohua Wang}
\affiliation{Center for Quantum Sciences and School of Physics, Northeast Normal University, Changchun 130024, China}

\author{Yan Zhang}
%\email{}
\affiliation{Center for Quantum Sciences and School of Physics, Northeast Normal University, Changchun 130024, China}

\date{\today}
\begin{abstract}
While ideal lattice models have been widely used to study giant-atom-lattice systems, they often neglect the disorder induced by defects during experimental fabrication. 
This work systematically explores the dynamics of a giant atom coupled to a disordered lattice characterized by random eigenfrequency fluctuations. 
By analyzing atomic excitation probabilities, photon dynamics, and energy spectra, we reveal that the system exhibits remarkable robustness when disorder is confined within a specific range. 
The atomic excitation probability remains stable, the lattice evolution process shows reproducibility, and the energy spectrum structure is minimally distorted.  
Notably, we introduce a quantitative parameter to measure non-Markovianity and demonstrate that disorder significantly influences the system’s memory effects. 
These findings provide insights into the interplay between disorder, non-Markovianity, and photon dynamics, with potential applications in quantum information processing and the design of robust quantum devices.



\end{abstract}

\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
\label{SecI}


In the realm of quantum optics, a profound understanding of the interaction between light and atoms has unveiled an important approximation methodology.
Besides the natural atoms, artificial atomic systems, which are engineered to possess energy-level structures similar to those of natural atoms, such as quantum dots~\cite{qd1} and superconducting qubits~\cite{sq1,sq2,sq3,sq4,sq5}, have been focused on for their atomic-like physical properties. 
Under the electric dipole approximation, these atoms are often idealized as point-like entities, although superconducting qubits are considerably larger than natural atoms due to their sizes being much smaller than the wavelengths of the microwave fields, referred to as the small atom.
Giant atoms with nonlocal coupling, as a novel quantum optical framework transcending the conventional dipole approximation, have been extensively investigated across various physical platforms, including superconducting quantum circuits~\cite{sqc1,sqc2}, coupled waveguide arrays~\cite{wa1}, Rydberg atoms~\cite{ra1,ra2,ra3}, and spin ensembles~\cite{se1}. 
These studies have unveiled a range of unique quantum phenomena arising from the self-interference effects of a single quantum emitter, such as frequency-dependent relaxation rates and Lamb shifts~\cite{ls1}, non-exponential decay characteristics~\cite{ned1,ned2}, decoherence-free interactions (DFI)~\cite{dfi1,dfi2,dfi3,dfi4}, oscillating bound state~\cite{obs1,obs2,obs3}, controllable frequency conversion~\cite{fc1,fc3,fc4}, long-lived quantum entanglement~\cite{qe1,qe2,qe3}, and stability-dependent dynamical behaviors. 
Unlike traditional atoms, giant atoms can couple to waveguides through multiple points. Therefore, when analyzing their dynamical properties, it is necessary to consider the accumulated phase of light propagation between these coupling points. 

%To date, most giant-atom schemes have been designed based on superconducting quantum circuits, primarily because natural atoms are much smaller than optical wavelengths, making it challenging to achieve the long-wavelength response required for giant-atom implementations. However, recently proposed synthetic giant-atom systems based on cold atoms, in both matter-wave and photonic configurations, offer new possibilities. These emerging platforms enable the design and manipulation of giant-atom phenomena over a broader parameter space, thereby holding promise for expanding the applications of giant atoms in the realms of quantum information and quantum optics.

Note that the non-Markovian retardation effect, resulting from the non-negligible propagation time of the quantum field between coupling points, significantly influences the dynamical properties of giant atoms ~\cite{nmm1,nm1,LeiDu12301}. 
The propagation time of light is often neglected in small atoms. 
%However, based on the Weisskopf-Wigner theory, when an atom emits in a non-characteristic continuous electromagnetic mode in vacuum, its spontaneous emission can be precisely described by the exponential decay of the atomic transition to the ground state, which is closely related to the irreversible emission of photons.
However, even if the atom-field coupling strength is extremely weak, significant non-Markovian dynamics~\cite{nm2,nm4,nm5,nm6,nm7,nm8,nm9,nm10} can still be exhibited by giant atoms. 
When $\tau$, a finite time $\tau$ for the light field or sound wave to propagate between the separated coupling points of the giant atom, is much smaller than the atomic relaxation time $\Gamma^{-1}$, the system is in the Markovian regime. 
When $\tau$ is comparable to $\Gamma^{-1}$, the giant atom enters the non-Markovian regime, and its evolution becomes dependent on the initial state of the system. 
Then, if energy is emitted from one coupling point and subsequently reabsorbed at another, this phenomenon is referred to as information reflux.
The environmental information reflux is a salient feature of non-Markovian dynamics~\cite{Seyed24201}. 
In recent years, non-Markovian delay effects have been confirmed in various systems, including geometrically large atomic systems~\cite{nmde1}, single atoms in front of a mirror~\cite{nmde2}, double-giant-cavity systems~\cite{nmde3}, macroscopically separated emitters~\cite{nmde4}, and linear chains of N-separated qubits~\cite{nmde5}, and a variety of numerical and analytical methods are employed, such as matrix product states~\cite{mps}, space-discretized waveguide models~\cite{dwm}, and diagrammatic approaches~\cite{da}. 
In the non-Markovian limit, the most typical behavior is that a giant atom can exhibit an unconventional oscillatory bound state~\cite{osbs}. Such oscillatory bound states emerge when a two-level giant atom couples to a one-dimensional continuous waveguide via three or more coupling points, arising from the coexistence of multiple bound states. In this regime, the atom can reciprocally exchange energy with the waveguide field. Additionally, introducing standard chiral couplings or effective chiral couplings induced by interference effects in giant atom systems can give rise to non-reciprocal frequency conversion~\cite{frc}, dark states without coherent driving~\cite{ds}, spontaneous chiral radiation~\cite{scr}, port-specific routers, and circulators~\cite{cir}.


To gain a deeper understanding of the physical phenomena arising from the non-Markovian properties of a giant atom coupled to a lattice, it is crucial to consider the structural defects that may inevitably be introduced during actual lattice fabrication, leading to lattice disorder. 
Here, we systematically explore the impact of lattice disorder on the non-Markovian dynamics of the giant atom, addressing a gap in the study of non-Markovian behavior in giant-atom-lattice systems with disorder.
In this paper, we investigate the dynamics of a giant atom interacting with a one-dimensional (1D) disordered lattice through two coupling points. 
The disorder in the system is characterized by irregular stochastic frequency fluctuations in each lattice unit, which significantly influence the system’s behavior.
A notable phenomenon observed in this process is the pronounced information reflux: within the single-excitation subspace of the Hilbert space, photons radiated into the lattice propagate bidirectionally. 
Specifically, the giant atom emits photons through one coupling point, while the other coupling point reabsorbs the energy, leading to the re-excitation of the atom.
Our findings reveal that the giant-atom excitation probability, photon evolution, and energy spectrum exhibit remarkable robustness against lattice disorder when the degree of disorder is tuned. 
However, the change in the disorder can effectively modulate the non-Markovian properties of the system, primarily reflected in significant changes in the non-Markovian measure.
These results demonstrate that, even when treating the lattice with inherent defects from experimental preparation as an ideal lattice, the theoretical predictions involving atomic evolution maintain a high degree of accuracy. 
However, due to the non-Markovian behavior arising from the multi-point coupling of giant atoms, it is found that the influence of disorder on non-Markovian measurements cannot be ignored. 
This highlights the role of disorder in modulating the non-Markovian properties of the system.

