\section{TWO-LEVEL GIANT ATOM}

\label{SecII}
\begin{figure}[tpb]
\includegraphics[width=8.5 cm]{Fig1.eps}
  \caption{Schematic configuration for a discrete lattice coupled to a giant atom at the sites $m$ and $n$.}
\label{F1}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{F1}, the giant atom system with two coupling points is composed of a one-dimensional discrete-waveguide lattice and an emitter with a ground state $\ket{g}$ and excited state $\ket{e}$. We model the waveguides as an array lattice of $z$=200 sites.
The system Hamiltonian $H$ is divided into three parts, i.e., $H=H_{0}+H_{j}+H_{I}$. 
$H_{0}$ is the free Hamiltonian of the giant atom as ($\hbar$ = 1)
\begin{equation}\label{eq_01}
H_{0}= \omega_{e}\ket{e} \bra{e},
\end{equation}
where $w_{e}$ is the transition frequency between states $\ket{e}$ and $\ket{g}$ with setting the frequency $\omega_{g} = 0$ of the ground state $\ket{g}$.

$H_{j}$, representing the free Hamiltonian of the lattice, is expressed as
\begin{equation}\label{eq_02}
{H}_{j}=\sum_{j}(\omega_{0}+\delta_{j}){a}_{j}^\dag{a}_{j}-J({a}_{j}^\dag{a}_{j+1}+{a}_{j+1}^\dag{a}_{j}),
\end{equation}
where $\omega_0$ represents the frequency of the waveguide mode, $J$ is the hopping strength between the nearest lattice sites, $\delta_{j}$ denotes the frequency fluctuation and the degree of disorder at the $j$ site, and $a_{j}\ (a_{j}^\dag)$ is the bosonic annihilation (creation) operator at the site $j$. 

$H_{I}$ describes the interaction between the lattices and the giant atom. 
Under the rotating wave approximation, $H_{I}$ is expressed as
\begin{equation}\label{eq_03}
H_{I}=(g_{m}a_{m}^\dag+g_{n}a_{n}^\dag)\sigma^{-}+(g_{m}a_{m}+g_{n}a_{n})\sigma^{+},
\end{equation}
where $\sigma^{+}=(\sigma^{-})^{\dag}=\ket{e} \bra{g}$ are Pauli operators of the giant atom.
Here, we have considered the giant atom coupled to the lattice at the site $m$ and the site $n$ with coupling strengths $g_{m}$ and $g_{n}$, respectively. 

Considering the whole system in a single-excitation subspace, we express the system wave function as
\begin{equation}\label{eq_04}
\ket{\psi(t)}=[\sum_{j}{C_{j}(t)a_{j}^{\dag}}+C_{e}(t)\sigma^{+}]\ket{0,g},
\end{equation}
where \(C_j(t)\) denotes the probability amplitude for a photon at site \(j\), \(C_e(t)\) represents the excitation amplitude of the giant atom, and \(\ket{0,g}\) describes the lattice in the vacuum state and the giant atom in its ground state \(\ket{g}\).


When system dynamics is decided by the Schr$\ddot{o}$dinger equation $i\ket{\dot{\psi}(t)}=H\ket{\psi(t)}$, we obtain the equations of motion of a giant atom and time evolution of the lattices, described as
\begin{equation}\label{eq_05}
\begin{split}
i\dot{C}_{j}(t)=&(\omega_{0}+\delta_{j})C_{j}(t)-JC_{j+1}(t)-JC_{j-1}(t)\\
&+g_{m}C_{e}(t)\delta_{j,m}+g_{n}C_{e}(t)\delta_{j,n},\\
i\dot{C}_{e}(t)=&\omega_{e}C_{e}(t)+g_{m}C_{m}(t)+g_{n}C_{n}(t).
\end{split}
\end{equation}

\section{REGULATION OF DYNAMICS BY DISORDER AND COUPLING POINTS SPACING}
\label{SecIII}

Conventional theoretical studies on atom-lattice coupling often assume an ideal lattice, yet experimental observations show that structural defects and disorder are inevitable in practical optical lattice systems.
We examine the time-evolution dynamics of a giant atom and photonic mode in a disordered lattice environment, revealing the mechanism by which lattice disorder influences the non-Markovianity measure in open quantum systems.

\subsection{The effect of disorder on atomic evolution and non-Markovian behavior}
\label{SecIIIA}

To quantify quantum non-Markovianity in open systems, several theoretical frameworks have been proposed. We utilize the measure \(N\) from~\cite{nmm1,nmm2}, defined by the volume of accessible states. 
The degree of quantum non-Markovianity is quantified by  
\begin{equation}\label{eq_12}
%\begin{split}
N_{V}= \int_{ \partial{t\left| C_{e}(t) \right|>0}}dt\frac{d\left| C_{e}(t) \right|^{4}}{dt}.
%\end{split}
\end{equation}
Non-Markovian behavior occurs if and only if the time derivative of \(\left| C_{e}(t) \right|\) is positive at some time.
That is, when the excitation probability of the giant atom increases at a certain time, the dynamical process corresponds to the non-Markovianity.
The increase of atomic excitation probability also corresponds to the occurrence of the information reflux phenomenon.
Specifically, \(N_{V} > 0\) if and only if \(\left| C_{e}(t) \right|\) increases at certain times. Conversely, if \(\left| C_{e}(t) \right|\) monotonically decreases, \(N_{V} = 0\), corresponding to Markovian behavior. 

To address the case of the strong coupling between atoms and the environment undergoing vacuum Rabi oscillations, we rescale the non-Markovianity measure using \(\left| C_{e}(\infty) \right| = 0\) and \(\left| C_{e}(0) \right| = 1\) as
\begin{equation}\label{eq_13}
\begin{split}
N=&\frac{N_{V}}{\left|\int_{\partial{t\left| C_{e}(t) \right|<0}}dt\frac{d\left| C_{e}(t) \right|^{4}}{dt}\right|}
=\frac{N_{V}}{N_{V}-\int^{\infty}_{0}dt\partial{t}\left| C_{e}(t) \right|^{4}}\\
=&\frac{N_{V}}{N_{V}+1}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Thus, in a Markovian region, the quantity \(\left| C_{e}(t) \right|\) monotonically decreases with time, leading to \(N = 0\).

