Figures~\figpanel{F2}{a} and ~\figpanel{F2}{b} depict the dynamical evolution characteristics of the giant atom under varying degrees of disorder.
With the coupling points positioned at \(m = 99\) and \(n = 102\) sites, figure~\figpanel{F2}{a} reveals that the decay process of the giant atom remains relatively stable and follows a consistent trend as the disorder strength \(\delta\) is adjusted from 0 to 0.02. 
However, a slight incomplete overlap of the three curves is observed. 
In the absence of disorder, the atomic excitation exhibits smooth exponential decay behavior, accompanied by minor oscillations attributed to the giant-atom self-interference effect. 
Due to the short giant-atom inner-coupling distance $|m-n|$ (distance between the two coupling points of the giant atom), information reflux phenomena occur before the atomic populations fully relax to the ground state. 
As the disorder strength increases, the decay behavior begins to exhibit noticeable fluctuations.

When $|m-n|$ is increased, figure~\figpanel{F2}{b} shows a significant difference in the system's behavior. 
With a sufficiently large $|m-n|$, the system exhibits an information backflow phenomenon after the atomic population decays to zero. 
Within the time interval \( Jt \in [0,20] \), the atomic excitation-state population probability decays exponentially. 
Since no secondary excitation events occur during this process, it represents a common spontaneous emission dynamics. 
Within the time interval \( Jt \in [20,40] \), the atom undergoes re-excitation by absorbing delayed photons from the other coupling site, leading to periodic revivals of its quantum state. 
Despite the presence of disorder, the decay behavior only exhibits fluctuations, indicating that the evolution of the atomic population is robust against the disorder in the lattice.

