Figures~\figpanel{F2}{c} and ~\figpanel{F2}{d} present the dynamical behavior of the non-Markovianity measure \( N \). 
The results clearly show that \( N \) exhibits a significant increasing trend with the enhancement of system disorder. 
Notably, the maximum peaks of \( N \) correspond to the information backflow regions observed in Figs.~\figpanel{F2}{c} and ~\figpanel{F2}{d}. 
When maintaining the same disorder parameter \( \delta = 0.02 \), a comparison of peak intensities between the two panels reveals that the non-Markovianity measure in Fig.~\figpanel{F2}{d} averages approximately \( 0.15 \sim 0.25 \) units higher than that in Fig.~\figpanel{F2}{c}. 
Further analysis confirms a direct positive correlation between the inner-coupling distance and the strength of non-Markovian effects, indicating that the system's non-Markovian behavior is significantly amplified as the giant-atom inner-coupling distance increases.
Consequently, the degree of disorder can evidently influence the non-Markovian dynamics of the system.


\subsection{Effect of coupling point spacing on evolution of giant atom and lattices in a disordered region}
\label{SecIIIB}



Figures~\figpanel{F3}{a}, ~\figpanel{F3}{c}, ~\figpanel{F3}{e}, and ~\figpanel{F3}{g} illustrate the temporal evolution characteristics of the atomic population and the lattice site during the interaction between the giant atom and the ordered lattice (\(\delta = 0\)).  
Figures~\figpanel{F3}{b}, ~\figpanel{F3}{d}, ~\figpanel{F3}{f}, and ~\figpanel{F3}{h} correspond to the scenario with disordered lattices (\(\delta = 0.02\)).  
For short inner-coupling distances, the atomic population exhibits quasi-exponential decay behavior, and the degree of disorder has no significant modulatory effect on the evolution trend as the inner-coupling distance increases.  
However, in Figs.~\figpanel{F3}{c} and~\figpanel{F3}{d}, for larger distances, the characteristic time required for atomic re-excitation increases significantly with increasing distance. 
This is attributed to the prolonged reabsorption time of delayed photons at the other coupling point due to the increased inner-coupling distance.  
%Notably, when the evolution time reaches $100$, the curves corresponding to larger intervals display periodic oscillations first, a phenomenon originating from photon scattering and reflection at the lattice boundaries (not shown here).
Comparing Figs.~\figpanel{F3}{e} with ~\figpanel{F3}{f}, and Figs.~\figpanel{F3}{g} with ~\figpanel{F3}{h}, it is evident that the evolution dynamics of photons at different \( j \) sites exhibit robustness against lattice disorder.  
The probability amplitude distribution of the lattice outside the coupling points exhibits exponential-like decay characteristics with high symmetry, while the evolution trajectories of the photon state in the lattice regions between the two coupling points reveal distinct spatiotemporal interference patterns.

