\section{The influence of disorder on energy spectrum}
\label{SecIV}

\begin{figure}[b]
\includegraphics[width=8.4 cm]{Fig5.eps}
\caption{\label{F5}
(a) and (b) Energy spectrum as a function of detuning \(\Delta = \omega_{0} - \omega_{e}\).  
(c) and (d) Energy spectrum as a function of hopping strength \(J\).  
(e) and (f) Energy spectrum as a function of coupling strength \(g\).  
(g) and (h) Energy spectrum as a function of coupling strength \(g\) with \(\omega_{0} = 2J\) and \(\omega_{e} = 5J\).  
Here, \(\delta = 0\) for (a), (c), (e), and (g), and \(\delta = -0.02 \sim 0.02\) for (b), (d), (f), and (h).  
The parameters \(m = 99\) and \(n = 102\) are used, and other parameters are the same as in Fig.~\ref{F2}.}
 \end{figure}




By optimizing the system parameter configuration, we demonstrate that the excitation energy can be confined within the bandgap region, where the propagation behavior of photons in the lattice is completely suppressed, thereby inducing a typical photon localization.  
Figure~\ref{F5} illustrates the energy of the lattice coupled to a giant atom as a function of detuning, hopping frequency, and coupling strength.  The left column corresponds to the ordered lattice system, while the right column corresponds to the disordered lattice system.  
A comparison of the results in the two columns reveals that while the energy band structures of the disordered and ordered lattices remain fundamentally consistent, the branches representing bound states in the energy spectra of the disordered system exhibit pronounced fluctuation behavior.  

Figures~\figpanel{F5}{a} and~\figpanel{F5}{b} demonstrate that the \(4J\)-wide energy band corresponds to the lattice-propagating mode of photons emitted by the giant atom (i.e., scattering states), while the upper and lower branches represent bound states, characterizing photon localization around the coupling points.  
Further analysis of Figs.~\figpanel{F5}{c} and~\figpanel{F5}{d} reveals that localized states emerge when the hopping strength \(J\) falls within the interval \([-0.1, 0.1]\).  
With \(\omega_{0} = 2J\) and \(\omega_{e} = 5J\), Figures~\figpanel{F5}{e} and~\figpanel{F5}{f} illustrate that the system exhibits bound state characteristics when the coupling strength \(g > 1J\).  
Combining with the results from Figs.~\figpanel{F5}{a} and~\figpanel{F5}{b}, photon localization occurs when \(|\Delta| > 2J\), and bound states persist across the entire parameter range of coupling strength \(g\).

\section{Conclusion}
\label{SecV}

In this work, we construct a disordered lattice model by introducing random fluctuations to the eigenfrequencies of each lattice site. 
Through numerical simulations, we analyze the time-resolved atomic excitation probabilities, photon dynamics, and energy spectra. 
Our results demonstrate that the system exhibits remarkable robustness against lattice disorder, with the giant-atom excitation probability and photon dynamics remaining largely unaffected.
However, the disorder significantly modulates the system’s non-Markovian properties, as evidenced by changes in the non-Markovian measure. 
This suggests that disorder can be used to tune non-Markovian behavior in experimental systems. Additionally, we showed that optimizing system parameters can confine excitation energy within the bandgap, inducing photon localization characterized by bound states.
These findings provide insights into the interplay between disorder, non-Markovianity, and photon dynamics in giant-atom systems, with potential applications in quantum information processing and robust quantum device design.

