									The Reynolds' operator $\pi^{\mathcal{G}}$ is the canonical ${\mathcal{G}}$-equivariant projection of an algebra $A$ onto an {isotypical} component of the trivial representation. Since ${\mathcal{G}}$ is linearly reductive, $\pi^{\mathcal{G}}$ commutes with all remaining ${\mathcal{G}}$-maps on $A$.  In particular the following equalities hold:
									\begin{eqnarray*}
										\pi^{\mathcal{G}}(ab)&=&a\pi^{\mathcal{G}}(b),\mbox{ if } a\in A^{\mathcal{G}}, b\in A\\
										\pi^{\mathcal{G}}(\mathrm{Trd\,}(a))&=& \mathrm{Trd\,}(\pi^{\mathcal{G}}(a)),\mbox{ if } a\in A.
									\end{eqnarray*}
									We refer the reader to the monograph \cite[pg. 154]{procesibookliegroup} for an
									extensive treatment of the topic. Lemma \ref{Berele223} implies that the Reynolds' operator property also holds for ${\mathcal{G}}$-maps on $A\otimes E$, i.e., the previous equalities are valid when we replace $\mathrm{Trd\,}$ by $ \mathrm{Strd}$.

