Random pixel permutations destroy spatial patterns, preventing CNN use and human classification. However, fully connected networks (like CoLaNET) find transformed images equally informative.

Sequential learning across ten MNIST permutations creates a degradation profile—a 10×10 matrix where element \(a_{k,j} \in [0, 1]\) represents classification accuracy on task j's test set after learning task k (\(j \leq k\)). From this profile we compute Average Accuracy (AA), Average Incremental Accuracy (AIA), Forgetting Measure (FM) and Backward Transfer (BWT) (Section \ref{sec:metrics}). 

\subsubsection{Baseline model}
\label{sec:ann}

We trained a fully connected ANN with 512 neurons in the hidden layer and 10 neurons in the output layer for classification as a baseline model \cite{Larionov2024b}. The network uses SGD with adaptive learning rate (AdaDelta) and achieves 98\% accuracy on the MNIST test set after 5 epochs. For CL, we set one epoch per task. This slightly reduces individual task performance (98\% to 96\%) but reduces inter-task interference and maintains online learning. %Specifically, when training with one epoch, the forgetting measure for the first task (after learning 9 subsequent tasks) was $96.81 - 49.43 = 47.38$, whereas with 5 epochs it was $98.10 - 34.48 = 63.62$ \cite{Larionov2024b}.

