\section{Conclusions}

%Based on the experiments conducted, we observed notable differences in performance between the Cartographer implementations utilizing the Ceres and g2o solvers.

%In the experiments with randomly generated point clouds, Ceres exhibited superior efficiency and performance compared to g2o. Ceres consistently required less time and fewer iterations to converge, with an average convergence time of 1.14 milliseconds and 13 iterations, compared to g2o's 1.59 milliseconds and 23 iterations. While both solvers produced similar pose adjustments, Ceres proved to be faster and more efficient overall.

%In real-world mapping scenarios using the AgileX LIMO ROS2 robot, Ceres generally generated clearer and more accurate maps, with sharper wall outlines and more accurate obstacle representation. The g2o implementation, on the other hand, showed weaker map closure at the endpoints and a slower overall map-building speed. However, g2o demonstrated better performance in localized areas, particularly in detecting specific obstacles. During the fixed path experiments, where the robot traveled at a consistent speed and duration, Ceres once again outperformed g2o in terms of map-building speed and overall completion of the laboratory environment. While g2o struggled with loop closure and exhibited slower map-building speeds, it excelled in certain areas of local obstacle detection, showcasing its strengths in specific conditions.

%Overall, while both solvers effectively optimize poses, Ceres consistently offers superior performance in terms of speed and convergence efficiency. However, g2o still has its advantages, particularly in localized scenarios where its obstacle detection capabilities outperform Ceres.

%It is important to acknowledge the impact of certain issues on the experimental results. Firstly, adaptability plays a significant role: both Ceres and Cartographer were developed by Google, ensuring inherent compatibility between them. Additionally, the Cartographer development team has meticulously optimized Ceres to support Cartographer's mapping, refining every detail for optimal performance. In contrast, our g2o implementation may not have achieved the same level of refinement due to limitations in resources and time, potentially affecting its overall performance. Secondly, errors and noise introduced by manually controlling experimental variables such as timing, speed, and route navigation could lead to potential inaccuracies. Although we conducted numerous trials to ensure that the experimental results accurately reflect the performance of the graph optimization algorithms, some level of uncertainty and noise remains inevitable in the process.

This study compared Ceres and g2o solvers within Cartographer’s scan matching module through synthetic and real-world experiments. Ceres consistently outperformed g2o in terms of speed and convergence, requiring fewer iterations and less time while achieving slightly better accuracy. In real-world tests with the AgileX LIMO robot, Ceres produced clearer and more complete maps, while g2o lagged in map closure and speed but showed strengths in localized obstacle detection. These results highlight Ceres’s overall efficiency and suitability for general SLAM tasks, whereas g2o may be advantageous in specific, localized scenarios.

It should be noted that Ceres benefits from native integration and extensive tuning within Cartographer, unlike our custom g2o implementation. Additionally, manual control during testing may have introduced minor variability, though repeated trials were conducted to ensure result consistency. Overall, this work offers practical insight into solver selection for SLAM systems and underscores the trade-offs between speed, accuracy, and adaptability when integrating alternative optimization backends.

Our future work includes further tuning of the g2o implementation, integrating other solvers such as GTSAM for broader comparison, and evaluating performance in more complex or dynamic environments. Additionally, automated benchmarking and real-time deployment on multi-robot systems could further validate the scalability and robustness of alternative solvers in SLAM pipelines.

